          The quietest generator is the one rated lowest at the load you will actually run, placed as far away as you can put it. A published 48 or 52 dBA is a best case at 23 feet and a quarter load. Here is how to read those numbers, and which generators publish the best ones in each size class.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-db-is-measured" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Generator Decibel Ratings Are Measured
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dBA number on a generator box is a single reading taken under conditions the manufacturer chose. The common convention is a sound meter 23 feet (7 meters) from the generator, with the generator running at 25% of its rated load. That is a reasonable test of a generator idling through the night with a refrigerator and a few lights on. It says very little about the same generator running a microwave or an air conditioner.
          </p>

          <p>
            The A in dBA means the reading is A-weighted, adjusted to reflect how human hearing works: it discounts very low and very high frequencies we hear less well. That matters because generators produce a lot of low-frequency engine rumble that A-weighting partly ignores. Two generators with the same dBA rating can sound different, one with more of a drone and one with more of a whine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful question for any rating is: at what distance, and at what load? Honda answers both for its EU series, publishing a quarter-load and a rated-load figure. Westinghouse gives 'as low as' figures without a stated load. WEN markets a quarter-load number. Some brands publish nothing. When we ranked the generators on this page, we compared like with like where we could and said so where we couldn't.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distance changes everything. In open air, sound drops by about 6 dB every time you double the distance from the source. A generator rated at 52 dBA at 23 feet will be around 46 dBA at 46 feet and around 58 dBA at 11 or 12 feet. That is why the same generator can seem quiet from a campsite picnic table and loud from the next tent over, and why a rating taken at 23 feet can't be compared with one taken at 10 feet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No load stated means assume quarter load. No distance stated means assume 23 feet.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">And treat the figure as the quietest the generator gets, not what it sounds like when it is working hard.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="log-scale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Log Scale: Why 10 dB Sounds Twice as Loud
          </h2>

          <p>
            Decibels are logarithmic, which makes small differences in the rating bigger than they look. A rise of 10 dB is roughly a doubling of perceived loudness. A rise of 3 dB doubles the sound energy but is close to the smallest change most people notice in ordinary conditions. A 5 dB difference is clearly noticeable side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Applied to the generators on this page: the Honda EU2200i at 48 dB(A) and a typical open-frame portable rated around 70 dBA are not 'about 30% apart'. The open-frame unit sounds roughly four times as loud. The gap between the Honda's quarter-load 48 and its rated-load 57 is close to a doubling, from the same machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            For reference, a quiet library is often put at around 40 dB, normal conversation at around 60 dB at a few feet, and a busy street at 70 to 80 dB. A generator rated near 50 dBA at 23 feet is quieter than two people talking near it. One rated near 70 is loud enough that you raise your voice to talk over it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
